<!-- ================= TOKYO ================= -->
<section class="city" data-c="tokyo" id="tokyo">
  <div class="cityhead"><h2>Tokyo</h2><span class="when">16–20 Sep · Cogo Ryōgoku</span></div>
  <p class="intro">Landing at 08:00 means day one is a real day, not a write-off. Ryōgoku sits on the JR Sōbu and Toei Ōedo lines, so almost everything below is one train or one easy transfer. Tap in and out with a Suica — no paper tickets needed.</p>

  <div class="day">Day 1 · Wed 16 Sep — Asakusa &amp; Skytree</div>

  <details class="stop">
    <summary><span class="sname">Sensō-ji Temple</span><span class="scost">free</span></summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p>Tokyo's oldest temple. Enter through Kaminarimon with its giant red lantern, walk Nakamise-dōri through the food and souvenir stalls, then the five-storey pagoda and main hall at the end. Busy by mid-morning — jet lag works in your favour here, go early.</p>
      <div class="route"><b>From Cogo Ryōgoku</b>Toei Ōedo Line to Kuramae, walk 4 min to Kuramae (Asakusa Line), then one stop to Asakusa. ~15 min · ¥220</div>
      <a class="maplink" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Senso-ji+Temple+Asakusa" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open in Maps</a>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="stop">
    <summary><span class="sname">Tokyo Skytree</span><span class="scost">¥2,100–3,500</span></summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p>Tallest tower in Japan. Tembo Deck at 350 m is the standard ticket; the Tembo Galleria at 450 m costs more and isn't essential. On a clear day you can see Fuji. Book a timed slot online to skip the queue — walk-up is pricier.</p>
      <div class="route"><b>From Sensō-ji</b>Walk 15–20 min across the river, or Tobu Skytree Line one stop. ~¥160</div>
      <a class="maplink" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Tokyo+Skytree" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open in Maps</a>
    </div>
  </details>

  <div class="day">Day 2 · Thu 17 Sep — Harajuku, Shibuya, Shinjuku</div>

  <details class="stop">
    <summary><span class="sname">Meiji Jingū</span><span class="scost">free</span></summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p>A forested Shinto shrine that swallows the noise of the city the moment you pass the torii. Fifteen-minute walk through the trees to the main hall. Get there before mid-morning or the crowds arrive with you.</p>
      <div class="route"><b>From Cogo Ryōgoku</b>Toei Ōedo Line to Shinjuku, transfer to JR Yamanote to Harajuku. ~35 min · ¥400</div>
      <a class="maplink" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Meiji+Jingu+Tokyo" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open in Maps</a>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="stop">
    <summary><span class="sname">Takeshita Street, Harajuku</span><span class="scost">food only</span></summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p>Narrow pedestrian street of teen fashion, thrift, crepes and novelty snacks. Twenty minutes is enough unless you're shopping. Omotesandō one street over is the calmer, more architectural version if the crowd gets old.</p>
      <div class="route"><b>From Meiji Jingū</b>Walk 5 min from the shrine's south exit.</div>
      <a class="maplink" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Takeshita+Street+Harajuku" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open in Maps</a>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="stop">
    <summary><span class="sname">Shibuya Crossing</span><span class="scost">free · Sky ¥2,500</span></summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p>The scramble everyone has seen in films. Worth crossing once, then watching from above — the free view is the Starbucks window at Tsutaya; the paid one is Shibuya Sky, an open rooftop deck with the best sunset view in the city. If you do one paid viewpoint in Tokyo, most people rate Shibuya Sky over Skytree.</p>
      <div class="route"><b>From Harajuku</b>JR Yamanote one stop to Shibuya, or walk 20 min down Meiji-dōri. ~¥150</div>
      <a class="maplink" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Shibuya+Crossing" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open in Maps</a>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="stop">
    <summary><span class="sname">Shinjuku after dark</span><span class="scost">food &amp; drink</span></summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p>Omoide Yokochō for grilled skewers in a lane of tiny smoke-filled counters, Golden Gai for closet-sized bars (some charge a cover for non-regulars — check the door), Kabukichō for the neon and the 3D cat billboard. Eat here rather than planning it around a single restaurant.</p>
      <div class="route"><b>From Shibuya</b>JR Yamanote, 2 stops. ~7 min · ¥160<br><b>Back to Ryōgoku</b>Toei Ōedo Line direct from Shinjuku. ~25 min · ¥280</div>
      <a class="maplink" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Omoide+Yokocho+Shinjuku" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open in Maps</a>
    </div>
  </details>

  <div class="day">Day 3 · Fri 18 Sep — Akihabara &amp; teamLab</div>

  <details class="stop">
    <summary><span class="sname">Akihabara Electric Town</span><span class="scost">free to browse</span></summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p>Multi-floor anime, manga, retro games and electronics. Super Potato for old consoles, Mandarake for second-hand everything, Yodobashi Camera if you want the enormous mainstream version. Arcades here are free to enter and cheap to play.</p>
      <div class="route"><b>From Cogo Ryōgoku</b>JR Sōbu Line, direct, no transfer. ~5 min · ¥170</div>
      <a class="maplink" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Akihabara+Electric+Town" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open in Maps</a>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="stop">
    <summary><span class="sname">teamLab Planets, Toyosu</span><span class="scost">¥3,800–4,200</span></summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p>Barefoot digital art museum — you wade through knee-deep water in some rooms, so wear shorts or trousers you can roll up. Allow 90 minutes inside. <strong>Book a timed ticket well in advance</strong>; it sells out weeks ahead and there is no walk-up.</p>
      <div class="route"><b>From Akihabara</b>Hibiya Line to Tsukiji, Yūrakuchō Line to Toyosu, then Yurikamome to Shin-Toyosu. ~40 min · ~¥450</div>
      <a class="maplink" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=teamLab+Planets+TOKYO" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open in Maps</a>
    </div>
  </details>

  <div class="day">Day 4 · Sat 19 Sep — Ueno, and a slower day</div>

  <details class="stop">
    <summary><span class="sname">Ueno Park</span><span class="scost">free · museums ¥1,000</span></summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p>Big open park with the Tokyo National Museum, the science museum, a zoo and Shinobazu Pond. Pick one museum rather than trying for several. Ameyoko market runs along the railway just south of the station — good for cheap street food.</p>
      <div class="route"><b>From Cogo Ryōgoku</b>JR Sōbu to Akihabara, JR Yamanote one stop to Ueno. ~12 min · ¥170</div>
      <a class="maplink" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Ueno+Park+Tokyo" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open in Maps</a>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="stop">
    <summary><span class="sname">Ryōgoku, on your doorstep</span><span class="scost">free–¥1,000</span></summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p>You're staying in the sumo district. The Kokugikan arena, the sumo museum, and chanko-nabe restaurants serving the stew wrestlers eat are all within a few minutes' walk. Worth an evening you don't spend on a train — and a good low-effort close to the Tokyo leg before Hakone.</p>
      <div class="route"><b>From Cogo Ryōgoku</b>On foot.</div>
      <a class="maplink" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Ryogoku+Kokugikan" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open in Maps</a>
    </div>
  </details>

  <div class="day">Money · Tokyo leg one</div>
  <div class="scroll">
  <table class="money">
    <tr><th>Item</th><th>Estimate</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Local trains, 4 days</td><td>¥3,500–4,500</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Tokyo Skytree</td><td>¥2,100–3,500</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Shibuya Sky (optional)</td><td>¥2,500</td></tr>
    <tr><td>teamLab Planets</td><td>¥3,800–4,200</td></tr>
    <tr><td>One museum in Ueno</td><td>¥1,000</td></tr>
    <tr class="total"><td>Transit + tickets</td><td>¥13,000–15,700</td></tr>
  </table>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ================= HAKONE ================= -->
<section class="city" data-c="hakone" id="hakone">
  <div class="cityhead"><h2>Hakone</h2><span class="when">20–22 Sep · 2 nights, 1 full day</span></div>
  <p class="intro">A mountain onsen town two hours from Tokyo. Two nights is the right length — enough for the loop and a proper soak, not so long that you run out of things to do.</p>

  <details class="stop">
    <summary><span class="sname">Getting there</span><span class="scost">¥2,500–3,500</span></summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p>Odakyū Romancecar from Shinjuku to Hakone-Yumoto, reserved seats, about 85 minutes. Book the seat in advance for a weekend departure.</p>
      <div class="route"><b>From Cogo Ryōgoku</b>Toei Ōedo Line direct to Shinjuku (~25 min, ¥280), then Romancecar.</div>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="stop">
    <summary><span class="sname">The Hakone loop</span><span class="scost">Free Pass ¥6,100</span></summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p>A circuit you do in one day using one pass: mountain railway to Gōra, cable car and ropeway over Ōwakudani's sulphur vents (buy a black egg, boiled in the hot springs), then a pirate-ship cruise across Lake Ashi with Fuji behind it if the weather holds, and a bus back. The Hakone Free Pass covers all of it.</p>
      <div class="route"><b>Start</b>Hakone-Yumoto station, first departure of the day beats the coach tours.</div>
      <a class="maplink" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Owakudani+Hakone" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open in Maps</a>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="stop">
    <summary><span class="sname">Onsen</span><span class="scost">¥1,000–2,000 day use</span></summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p>The whole point of stopping here. Many ryokan open their baths to day visitors; if your lodging has its own, use it twice. Note that a number of traditional baths still refuse entry to visible tattoos — check before you go, or look for a private <em>kashikiri</em> bath you book by the hour.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="stop">
    <summary><span class="sname">Hakone Open-Air Museum</span><span class="scost">¥1,600</span></summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p>Sculpture park in the mountains with a Picasso pavilion and a foot bath fed by a hot spring. Easy to fold into the loop — it's a stop on the mountain railway at Chōkoku-no-mori.</p>
      <a class="maplink" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Hakone+Open-Air+Museum" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open in Maps</a>
    </div>
  </details>
</section>

<!-- ================= KYOTO ================= -->
<section class="city" data-c="kyoto" id="kyoto">
  <div class="cityhead"><h2>Kyoto</h2><span class="when">22–26 Sep · 4 nights</span></div>
  <p class="intro">The longest single stop of the trip, and it needs to be. Kyoto's sights are spread across the edges of the city rather than clustered downtown, so group them by direction: east one day, west another, south another.</p>

  <details class="stop">
    <summary><span class="sname">Getting there</span><span class="scost">~¥13,000</span></summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p>Bus or train down from Hakone to Odawara, then Tōkaidō Shinkansen straight to Kyoto — about 2 hours 15 from Odawara. Reserve a seat with luggage space if you're carrying a large bag.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="stop">
    <summary><span class="sname">Fushimi Inari Taisha</span><span class="scost">free</span></summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p>Thousands of vermilion torii gates winding up a mountainside. The crowds thin dramatically after the first twenty minutes of climbing, and the full loop to the summit takes 2–3 hours. Open 24 hours — sunrise or after dark is when you get it to yourself.</p>
      <div class="route"><b>From Kyoto Station</b>JR Nara Line, 2 stops to Inari. ~5 min · ¥150</div>
      <a class="maplink" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Fushimi+Inari+Taisha" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open in Maps</a>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="stop">
    <summary><span class="sname">Arashiyama &amp; the bamboo grove</span><span class="scost">free · temple ¥500</span></summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p>Western edge of the city. The bamboo path is short and extremely busy after 9am — go at opening. While you're out there: Tenryū-ji's garden, the Iwatayama monkey park up the hill, and the Hozu river.</p>
      <div class="route"><b>From Kyoto Station</b>JR Sagano/San-in Line to Saga-Arashiyama. ~17 min · ¥240</div>
      <a class="maplink" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Arashiyama+Bamboo+Grove" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open in Maps</a>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="stop">
    <summary><span class="sname">Higashiyama: Kiyomizu-dera &amp; Gion</span><span class="scost">¥500</span></summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p>Do this as one long walk. Kiyomizu-dera's wooden terrace over the hillside, then downhill through the preserved lanes of Sannenzaka and Ninenzaka, through Yasaka Shrine and into Gion for the evening. This is where you're most likely to see geiko and maiko heading to appointments around dusk — watch, don't chase.</p>
      <div class="route"><b>From Kyoto Station</b>City bus 100 or 206 to Kiyomizu-michi. ~15 min · ¥230</div>
      <a class="maplink" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Kiyomizu-dera" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open in Maps</a>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="stop">
    <summary><span class="sname">Kinkaku-ji</span><span class="scost">¥500</span></summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p>The gold pavilion over a reflecting pond. It's a fifteen-minute visit on a fixed one-way path and it is always crowded, but the image is the reason people come to Kyoto. Pair it with Ryōan-ji's rock garden nearby to make the trip north worthwhile.</p>
      <div class="route"><b>From Kyoto Station</b>City bus 101 or 205. ~40 min · ¥230</div>
      <a class="maplink" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Kinkaku-ji" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open in Maps</a>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="stop">
    <summary><span class="sname">Day trip: Nara</span><span class="scost">¥1,800 return</span></summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p>Free-roaming deer that bow for crackers, and Tōdai-ji, which holds a 15-metre bronze Buddha in the largest wooden building you'll ever stand in. Half a day is enough, and it's the best use of your fourth Kyoto day.</p>
      <div class="route"><b>From Kyoto Station</b>JR Nara Line rapid, direct. ~45 min · ¥720 each way</div>
      <a class="maplink" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Nara+Park+Todai-ji" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open in Maps</a>
    </div>
  </details>
</section>

<!-- ================= OSAKA ================= -->
<section class="city" data-c="osaka" id="osaka">
  <div class="cityhead"><h2>Osaka</h2><span class="when">26–29 Sep · 3 nights, 2 full days</span></div>
  <p class="intro">Loud, unpretentious, and built around eating. After four days of temples this is the palate cleanser. Only 15 minutes from Kyoto by Shinkansen, or half an hour on a regular train.</p>

  <details class="stop">
    <summary><span class="sname">Dōtonbori</span><span class="scost">food only</span></summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p>Canal-side strip under the Glico running man sign — takoyaki, okonomiyaki, kushikatsu (never double-dip the sauce). Best after dark when the signs are lit. Adjoining Hōzenji Yokochō is a quiet stone alley one street over if you need a break from it.</p>
      <div class="route"><b>From Osaka Station</b>Midōsuji subway to Namba. ~8 min · ¥240</div>
      <a class="maplink" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Dotonbori+Osaka" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open in Maps</a>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="stop">
    <summary><span class="sname">Osaka Castle</span><span class="scost">¥600</span></summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p>Reconstructed keep in a large moated park. The interior is a museum with a lift, so it's more history exhibit than castle — the grounds and the view from the top floor are the draw.</p>
      <div class="route"><b>From Osaka Station</b>JR Osaka Loop Line to Osakajō-kōen. ~11 min · ¥180</div>
      <a class="maplink" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Osaka+Castle" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open in Maps</a>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="stop">
    <summary><span class="sname">Kuromon Ichiba Market</span><span class="scost">food only</span></summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p>Covered market of seafood, fruit and grilled skewers, much of it cooked in front of you. Go hungry and mid-morning; stalls start closing in the late afternoon.</p>
      <div class="route"><b>From Namba</b>Walk 5 min.</div>
      <a class="maplink" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Kuromon+Ichiba+Market" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open in Maps</a>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="stop">
    <summary><span class="sname">Options for the second day</span><span class="scost">varies</span></summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p>Umeda Sky Building's floating observatory for the skyline, Shinsekai for retro neon and kushikatsu, or day-trip to Kobe (30 min) for beef and a harbour. Universal Studios Japan is here too if that's your thing — but it eats an entire day and needs an advance ticket.</p>
    </div>
  </details>
</section>

<!-- ================= HIROSHIMA ================= -->
<section class="city" data-c="hiroshima" id="hiroshima">
  <div class="cityhead"><h2>Hiroshima</h2><span class="when">29 Sep–2 Oct · 3 nights, 2 full days</span></div>
  <p class="intro">Two full days so you don't have to choose between the city and Miyajima. Give the Peace Park a morning at least — it deserves more time than people usually plan for.</p>

  <details class="stop">
    <summary><span class="sname">Getting there</span><span class="scost">~¥10,500</span></summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p>Sanyō Shinkansen from Shin-Osaka to Hiroshima, about 90 minutes on a Nozomi.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="stop">
    <summary><span class="sname">Peace Memorial Park &amp; Museum</span><span class="scost">¥200</span></summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p>The A-Bomb Dome, the cenotaph, the children's memorial hung with paper cranes, and a museum that is deliberately difficult. Allow three hours and don't schedule something demanding straight afterwards. The museum entry is almost free by design.</p>
      <div class="route"><b>From Hiroshima Station</b>Tram line 2 or 6 to Genbaku Dome-mae. ~15 min · ¥240</div>
      <a class="maplink" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Hiroshima+Peace+Memorial+Park" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open in Maps</a>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="stop">
    <summary><span class="sname">Miyajima &amp; the floating torii</span><span class="scost">~¥1,000 + ¥300 shrine</span></summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p>Island in the bay where the great torii stands in the water at high tide and you can walk out to it at low tide — check the tide table before you pick your hour. Itsukushima Shrine sits on stilts over the same water, wild deer wander the streets, and the ropeway up Mt Misen gives you the inland sea. A full day.</p>
      <div class="route"><b>From Hiroshima Station</b>JR Sanyō Line to Miyajimaguchi (~27 min, ¥420), then the JR ferry across (~10 min, ¥200). Plus a ¥100 island visitor tax.</div>
      <a class="maplink" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Itsukushima+Shrine+Miyajima" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open in Maps</a>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="stop">
    <summary><span class="sname">Okonomiyaki, Hiroshima style</span><span class="scost">¥900–1,400</span></summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p>Layered rather than mixed, with noodles inside — a different dish from the Osaka version and worth eating both to compare. Okonomimura is a building of stacked okonomiyaki counters near the Peace Park.</p>
      <a class="maplink" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Okonomimura+Hiroshima" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open in Maps</a>
    </div>
  </details>
</section>

<!-- ================= TOKYO 2 ================= -->
<section class="city" data-c="tokyo2" id="tokyo2">
  <div class="cityhead"><h2>Tokyo again</h2><span class="when">2–6 Oct · 4 nights, 3 full days</span></div>
  <p class="intro">Back on the Shinkansen — about four hours direct from Hiroshima. Three real days here, because the 6th is a travel day. This is the leg for whatever the first Tokyo stint didn't fit, plus the shopping you didn't want to carry around the country.</p>

  <details class="stop">
    <summary><span class="sname">Tsukiji Outer Market</span><span class="scost">food only</span></summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p>The wholesale auction moved to Toyosu, but the outer market is still here and it's the better visit — tamagoyaki on a stick, uni, knives, dried goods. Go before 10am; a lot of it shuts by early afternoon.</p>
      <div class="route"><b>From Ryōgoku</b>Toei Ōedo Line direct to Tsukijishijō. ~15 min · ¥220</div>
      <a class="maplink" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Tsukiji+Outer+Market" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open in Maps</a>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="stop">
    <summary><span class="sname">Shimokitazawa</span><span class="scost">free to browse</span></summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p>Low-rise neighbourhood of second-hand clothing, record shops and small cafés, with almost no cars. The antidote to Shibuya — good for a slow afternoon near the end of a long trip.</p>
      <div class="route"><b>From Ryōgoku</b>Ōedo Line to Shinjuku, Odakyū Line to Shimokitazawa. ~40 min · ¥450</div>
      <a class="maplink" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Shimokitazawa+Tokyo" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open in Maps</a>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="stop">
    <summary><span class="sname">Yanaka</span><span class="scost">free</span></summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p>One of the few districts that survived both the 1923 earthquake and the war, so the streets are old and low. Yanaka Ginza shopping street, a large cemetery full of cherry trees, cats everywhere. Quiet in a way central Tokyo isn't.</p>
      <div class="route"><b>From Ryōgoku</b>JR Sōbu to Akihabara, Yamanote to Nippori. ~20 min · ¥180</div>
      <a class="maplink" href="https://www.google.com/maps/search/?api=1&amp;query=Yanaka+Ginza+Tokyo" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Open in Maps</a>
    </div>
  </details>

  <details class="stop">
    <summary><span class="sname">Held in reserve</span><span class="scost">varies</span></summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p>Things worth keeping for this leg rather than forcing into September: Ghibli Museum (ticket released on a fixed date a month ahead, sells out in minutes), the Imperial Palace East Gardens, Odaiba, a day trip to Kamakura for the Great Buddha and the coast, Ginza for department-store food halls.</p>
    </div>
  </details>

  <div class="day">Tue 6 Oct · getting to Narita</div>

  <details class="stop">
    <summary><span class="sname">Ryōgoku → Narita Terminal 1</span><span class="scost">¥1,300–3,000</span></summary>
    <div class="body">
      <p>ZIPAIR departs 14:35, so be airside by around 12:15 and leave Ryōgoku by <strong>10:00–10:30</strong>. That makes the 6th a checkout-and-travel day — the last real morning in Tokyo is the 5th. ZIPAIR flies from Terminal 1, not the Terminal 2 you arrive into.</p>
      <div class="route"><b>Fastest</b>JR Sōbu to Akihabara, Yamanote to Nippori, then Keisei Skyliner to Narita. ~75 min · ~¥2,800<br><b>Simplest with bags</b>JR Narita Express from Tokyo Station, no transfers after that. ~80 min · ~¥3,000<br><b>Cheapest</b>Keisei Access Express via Asakusa Line — one transfer, no reservation. ~95 min · ~¥1,300</div>
    </div>
  </details>
</section>

<p class="note">Prices are 2026 estimates in yen and will drift — treat them as planning figures, not quotes. Train times assume a Suica or Pasmo card rather than paper tickets. Anything with a timed entry (teamLab Planets, Shibuya Sky, Ghibli Museum, Shinkansen reserved seats) should be booked before you fly.</p>

